WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:03.363
- [Narrator] They begin life as ghosts,

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:08.970
gently coursing through
a solitary existence,

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:13.870
but slowly, their gentility turns to rage.

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:17.940
They grow larger and larger,
hurling and twisting,

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:21.200
and desperately reaching
down from the sky,

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:24.320
and what began as an invisible shade

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:27.975
is turned into a monster.

00:00:27.975 --> 00:00:31.170
(foreboding music)

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:34.740
Tornadoes are powerful
spinning columns of air

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:37.813
that stretch from the
ground to the clouds.

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:40.670
Most are relatively weak,

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:43.100
but the few that grow into large events

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:46.543
are extremely violent and
cause immense destruction.

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:51.940
Tornadoes occur on six
of the seven continents.

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:55.190
The country with the most
tornadoes based on land size

00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:58.230
is the United Kingdom
with an average of about

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:01.133
33 tornadoes reported each year.

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:04.050
But the country with the greatest overall

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:07.330
number of tornadoes and the most intense

00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:11.593
is the United States with
over 1,000 reported annually.

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:16.360
Tornadoes, no matter where they occur,

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:19.980
are classified as either
supercell tornadoes,

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:22.080
which form within supercells,

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:25.070
the most powerful class of thunderstorms,

00:01:25.070 --> 00:01:27.280
or non-supercell tornadoes,

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:29.150
which are smaller and weaker

00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:32.183
and form within non-supercell storms.

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:35.160
There are many theories surrounding

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:37.500
the formation of tornadoes.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:39.220
One key component they share

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:42.210
is the presence of both
high and low pressure air

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:43.830
in a given space.

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:46.470
Air particles from the
area of high pressure

00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:49.070
move toward an area of low pressure,

00:01:49.070 --> 00:01:51.463
a movement that creates wind.

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:54.620
Non-supercell tornadoes,

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:57.310
such as waterspouts and landspouts,

00:01:57.310 --> 00:01:59.750
begin when cool high-pressure air

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:02.490
and warm low-pressure air are present,

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:05.200
particularly near ground level.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:07.610
As air particles move horizontally

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:10.940
from the high pressure area
to the low pressure area,

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:13.340
wind begins to pick up.

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:15.290
Winds blowing at different speeds

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:17.780
and in different directions and altitudes

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:20.240
begin to blow cyclically.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:22.680
In the case of non-supercell tornadoes,

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:25.763
they turn into an upright spinning vortex.

00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:28.950
But to create supercell tornadoes,

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:31.970
the circumstances are slightly different.

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:35.830
Violent supercell storms
draw warm low-pressure air

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:37.670
up to a higher altitude,

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:41.163
leaving behind cool high-pressure
air near the ground.

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:43.660
Air particles attempting to bring

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:46.320
the two levels of air
pressure into balance

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:49.490
creates wind that blows vertically.

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:53.470
The wind increases and starts
to blow in a cyclical fashion,

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:56.823
creating a pipe of wind
that rolls along the ground.

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:02.480
In both cases, an upward current
of wind called an updraft

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:05.970
provides the final ingredient
for creating a tornado.

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:08.450
In a budding non-supercell tornado,

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:11.210
an updraft stretches its vertical vortex

00:03:11.210 --> 00:03:13.610
until it reaches the clouds.

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:15.770
To create a supercell tornado,

00:03:15.770 --> 00:03:18.820
an updraft lifts the
rolling pipe of wind upward

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:21.120
until it stands upright.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:23.780
Then it pulls condensation from the skies

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:26.480
and into the spinning vortex.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:30.180
As soon as the vortices,
supercell or non-supercell,

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:32.330
connect the ground to the clouds,

00:03:32.330 --> 00:03:35.493
they are officially
classified as tornadoes.

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:40.440
All tornadoes are rated
based on a system called

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:42.650
the Enhanced Fujita Scale.

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:45.660
The Enhanced Fujita, or EF, Scale,

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:50.193
classifies tornadoes from
a rating of EF0 to EF5.

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:53.520
The rating is based on
a number of factors,

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:55.960
such as the damage a tornado causes

00:03:55.960 --> 00:03:59.620
and the Doppler radar
estimates of its wind speeds.

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:02.210
EF0 tornadoes are the weakest,

00:04:02.210 --> 00:04:06.820
with the wind speeds between
65 to 85 miles per hour.

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:09.460
EF5 tornadoes are the strongest,

00:04:09.460 --> 00:04:13.043
with the wind speeds
exceeding 200 miles per hour.

00:04:15.430 --> 00:04:17.520
One of the strongest tornadoes recorded

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:21.193
occurred in Oklahoma
City, Oklahoma in 1999.

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:24.660
Born from a supercell thunderstorm,

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:29.623
the EF5 tornado had wind speeds
of over 300 miles per hour.

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:35.770
It resulted in 36 fatalities,
injured nearly 600,

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:39.197
and caused about $1 billion in damages.

00:04:39.197 --> 00:04:41.974
(somber music)

00:04:41.974 --> 00:04:43.020
(dramatic music)

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:45.480
While tornadoes cannot be prevented,

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:48.323
measures are being taken
to protect communities.

00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:52.220
Meteorologists closely
monitor storm fronts

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:55.100
in high-risk areas and try to forecast

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:57.220
possible tornadic events.

00:04:57.220 --> 00:05:00.970
In doing so, they help mitigate
damages to neighborhoods

00:05:00.970 --> 00:05:03.250
and save countless lives,

00:05:03.250 --> 00:05:06.183
even in the face of one of
nature's most formidable.

